<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import OfflineBanner from './lib/OfflineBanner.abide'
import offlineBannerSource from './lib/OfflineBanner.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <p>You need two things: a way to tell them, and a way to catch up when the connection
    returns.</p>

    <Section title="The forms of `online`">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>What you get</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>online</code></td><td>a <code>Reactive&lt;boolean&gt;</code> — whether the caller can reach the app</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>online()</code></td><td>the same value, spelled explicitly</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>There is one form and no options, <code>online</code> being ambient rather than declared:
        it is not constructed, has no producer to reload and takes nothing to key on. What it is on
        each side is <a href={url('/reference/ambient-values')}>Ambient values</a>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`online` is a reactive value">
        <Example
            id="online-banner"
            title="`online` — the connection, read by name"
            summary="An order form with unsaved changes. Take the browser offline — DevTools' Network panel, or the Wi-Fi — and watch the line."
            files={{
                'lib/OfflineBanner.abide': offlineBannerSource,
            }}
        >
            <OfflineBanner />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 name, 0 listeners</em> — against <code>online</code> and <code>offline</code>
        handlers and a flag kept in step between them.</p>

        <p>Read it by name, like any other. There is no listener to attach and no cleanup to
        remember. Reading it subscribes, and the paragraph mounts and unmounts as the answer
        moves.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`online` answers whether the caller can reach the app">
        <p>One question, asked the same way on both sides and answered from what each side has.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Side</th><th>Answered from</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>browser</td><td><code>navigator.onLine</code> and its events</td></tr>
                <tr><td>server</td><td>the request that arrived — a caller being served has reached the app</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>So a document rendered for a client that dropped in the meantime says <code>true</code>,
        and the client's own first read corrects it. That correction is an ordinary value change,
        not a hydration mismatch: nothing is rebuilt, the text updates where it stands.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Catch up by refreshing, not invalidating">
        <p>When the connection comes back, what you want reloaded is whatever the reader is actually
        looking at — not every value the scope is holding.</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/layout.abide — excerpt" code={`<script>
import { online, refresh, watch } from '@abide/abide'

watch(() => { if (online) refresh() })
</script>`} />

        <p><code>refresh()</code> is bounded by what is <strong>subscribed</strong> rather than by
        the scope: it reloads the entries something is reading and degenerates to a mark for the
        rest. So a scope holding two hundred catches up the handful a reader is holding and marks
        the other hundred and ninety-odd, which is exactly the line above and makes it the reconnect
        default. The watch also runs once as the layout mounts, with <code>online</code> already
        true, so what is subscribed then reloads once as well.</p>

        <p><code>invalidate()</code> is the other choice, for where the values on screen must stay:
        it runs nothing, sends nothing and wakes nobody (7.3, 7.14), and leaves every value standing
        until the next read or probe of it runs its producer (7.17). It is not a cheaper
        <code>refresh()</code> to reach for first — the read or the <code>refresh()</code> that
        follows one loads over a value marked wrong, which reports <code>ready()</code> false (7.8),
        and every reader guarding on it drops to its pending branch.</p>

        <p>See <a href={url('/values/decide-when-a-value-reloads')}>Reloading</a> for the two in
        full.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`navigator.onLine` is a weak signal, and only in one direction">
        <p><code>false</code> is trustworthy: the interface is down, and nothing is going anywhere.
        <code>true</code> is a guess — it says the interface is up, not that your server answers, so
        a captive portal, a DNS failure and a dead backend all read as online.</p>

        <p>That shapes what <code>online</code> is good for. It is right for telling a reader what
        to expect and wrong as the gate on a write: the load that failed is the honest signal, and
        <code>online</code> explains it. Read the failure, then read <code>online</code> to say
        why.</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/orders/index.abide — excerpt" code={`{#if orders.error()}
    <p>{online ? 'Could not load orders.' : 'You are offline.'}</p>
{/if}`} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a> — what
            a page shows before a value lands</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/decide-when-a-value-reloads')}>Reloading</a> —
            <code>invalidate</code> against <code>refresh</code>, over one value or a selection</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/do-something-when-a-value-changes')}>Watching values</a> —
            what <code>watch</code> runs, and when it stops</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
